  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days...\n\nThe rest of today and most of Tuesday will come with the familiar\nbenign weather, although a stray shower or two is possible. Mid to\nhigh level clouds are forecast to linger around though, especially\nnear and west of Billings.\n\nDuring the day Tuesday an upper level low will progress out of the\nGreat Basin and through Wyoming. Because of the Pacific source\nregion this system will bring with it some pretty good moisture,\neven after a majority of it is used up in the mountains along the\nway. The HRRR is depicting PWAT values across our CWA around 0.5\"\nwhile the REFS has pockets of 0.75\". This will be plenty of \nmoisture to work with as a surface low develops over western WY \nand moves NE through SE MT. As of the latest runs, it does appear \nthat the low will be too far northwest for there to be a good \nsetup for upslope snow on the backside into Sheridan and Red \nLodge. That being said though, we will see good precip totals for \nmid November. The NBM is showing widespread 0.1\" to 0.2\" total \nliquid along and west of a KSHR to KMLS line. \n\nUnfortunately for snow lovers, the temperatures do appear to be a\nlittle too warm for snow below 5kft. Locations in the foothills \nof south-central Montana could pick up a couple inches still; with\nlocations such as Livingston and Judith Gap most likely to see\naccumulations. Outside of that, the only locations not in the \nmountains that have a chance at accumulating snow with this event \nwill be in the highest locations of the plains, such as the Wolf \nMountains. A Winter Weather Advisory was issued for Park Co and \nthe Judith Gap area due to the forecast snowfall in addition to \nthe blustery winds which could create hazardous travel Tuesday \nevening through Wednesday morning. The greatest risk to travel \nwill certainly be along I-90 over the Bozeman Pass where 3-6' of\nsnow and windy conditions are forecast.\n\nSpeaking of winds, as the low pressure system moves through our\neastern zones and into the Dakotas Wednesday, windy conditions \nare forecast. As alluded to above, the typical gap prone areas \nsuch as Harlowton and Big Timber will see some of the strongest \ngusts where observations in the 50s mph are possible. The \ndeepening low will also create breezy winds closer to its core \nover Eastern Montana. 40s and 50s mph gusts in Baker and Ekalaka \nare also forecast during the day Wednesday which could cause \ntravel problems for high profile vehicles. \n\nAs the midweek system moves out of the area, some weak ridging\nwill build in Thursday. While this will not do much to rebound\ntemperatures, it will briefly dry out the region before the next\nround of precip. Thursday AM could come with areas of fog though as\nclear skies and calm winds will promote fog development in\nconjunctions with the increased surface moisture. The areal \nextent and density of the fog will depend on how much total precip\nfalls and how fast the system exits Wednesday night. \n\nThe next weather maker will move into the region overnight \nThursday into Friday. There is still a large amount of \ndisagreement amongst models regarding how exactly this event will \nplay out. The ECMWF, GFS, and their ensembles continue to have \nslight differences that could have large implications. As of now a\nmajority of the members take the system a touch too far south for\nour CWA to see impactful precipitation but this is far from a \nguarantee. What is more certain though is that temps will be cold \nenough that nearly all of us will see snow with this event if any \nprecip were to fall. Although the track of the low is trending \nsouth recently, most members of the long range ensembles give \nBillings its first accumulating snowfall of the season. Those who\nhave plans this weekend should check future forecasts as details\ncome more into focus. WMR",
